// The SUBSTITUTION route's copy of the subsumed-arm collapse — `pick<T>(v: T): T | "err"`
// at `T = string`. silent-class-inventory D1049, filed the same day D1024 shipped because
// D1024 reached only TWO of the three union-construction sites.
//
// `annUnionInnerTy` (both annotation spellings) and `fillTypeDeclAt`'s `UnionDecl` arm call
// the shared `unionDropSubsumedArms`; a union minted by `substTyDeep` at the pin reached
// neither, so the generic spelling still minted the duplicate `string` atom and was
// `vl check` rc 0 followed by `expected struct type at index 0, found (array (mut i32))` —
// D1024's message, byte for byte — while the DIRECT spelling
// `function pick(v: string): string | "err"` ran and printed `x`.
//
// THE `is` ARM IS THE HALF THAT MUST DISCRIMINATE, not merely compile. `T | "err"` at
// `T = string` IS `string`, so `r is "err"` is a value test over a bare string ref: it has
// to answer FALSE for `"x"` and TRUE for `"err"`. A collapse that produced a rep the `is`
// could not read would still print something.
//
// THE NON-COLLAPSING INSTANTIATIONS ARE NOT HERE, for the same reason D1024's probe left
// its litunion control out: `T = i32` and `T = Circle` are genuine two-arm boxes minted at
// the pin, and such a box has no `is` lowering yet (D1042 — `union box atom test on a union
// with no recorded members: i32|"err"`). They are pinned instead as a CHECKER fact in
// `tests/cases/generics/error-subsumed-literal-arm-at-the-pin-controls.vl`, where
// `const z: i32 = pick(7)` stays a type error exactly while the `"err"` arm survives.
//
// Should print x then V then E.

function pick<T>(v: T): T | "err" { return v }

print(pick("x"))
const r0 = pick("x")
if r0 is "err" { print("E") } else { print("V") }
const r1 = pick("err")
if r1 is "err" { print("E") } else { print("V") }
